            "theoryHtml": "\n        <h4>The Transition to Sovereign Sultanate (1206 AD)</h4>\n        <p>Following the assassination of Muhammad Ghori in March 1206 at Dhamyak, his empire fractured among his senior Turkish slave commanders (<em>Mamluks</em>):</p>\n        <ul>\n            <li><strong>Tajuddin Yaldiz:</strong> Seized Ghazni and claimed sovereign suzerainty over all Ghorid conquests.</li>\n            <li><strong>Nasiruddin Qabacha:</strong> Established himself as the independent master of Multan, Uch, and Sindh.</li>\n            <li><strong>Ikhtiyaruddin Bakhtiyar Khilji:</strong> Controlled Bihar and Bengal (dying the same year, 1206 AD).</li>\n            <li><strong>Qutbuddin Aibak:</strong> Stationed at Lahore, was requested by the citizens and nobles of Lahore to assume supreme authority over the Indian territories. In June 1206 AD, Aibak formally ascended the throne at <strong>Lahore</strong>.</li>\n        </ul>\n\n        <h4>Qutbuddin Aibak (1206–1210 AD): The First Mamluk Ruler</h4>\n        <p>Aibak, born of Turkic stock in Turkistan, was sold as a slave in boyhood to the chief Qazi of Nishapur, who educated him in Islamic jurisprudence, archery, and horsemanship, before being purchased by Muhammad Ghori:</p>\n        <ul>\n            <li><strong>Constitutional Status:</strong> Aibak did not strike coins in his own name or read the Friday sermon (<em>Khutba</em>) in his name. He ruled modestly with the subordinate titles of <strong>Malik</strong> and <strong>Sipahsalar</strong> (Commander-in-Chief).</li>\n            <li>In 1208 AD, Ghori's nephew and successor at Ghor, Ghiyasuddin Mahmud, formally sent Aibak royal insignia—the canopy (<em>Chatr</em>), kettle-drums (<em>Naubat</em>), and a deed of manumission (freeing him from legal enslavement)—conferring the title of <em>Sultan</em>.</li>\n            <li><strong>Reputation for Philanthropy:</strong> Due to his extraordinary generosity and distribution of gifts to scholars and the needy, Aibak was given the popular title <strong>Lakhbaksh</strong> ('Giver of Lakhs').</li>\n            <li>He patronized distinguished scholars including <strong>Hasan Nizami</strong>, author of the premier court chronicle <em>Taj-ul-Ma'asir</em>, and Fakhr-i-Mudabbir (author of <em>Adab-ul-Harb wash-Shuja'at</em>).</li>\n        </ul>\n\n        <h4>Tragic Demise (1210 AD)</h4>\n        <p>Aibak's brief four-year reign was dedicated entirely to maintaining peace and checking the ambitions of Yaldiz and Qabacha from his headquarters at Lahore. In <strong>1210 AD</strong>, while playing <strong>Chaugan</strong> (medieval polo) on horseback at Lahore, he fell from his mount and was fatally impaled on the high pommel of his saddle. He was buried in Anarkali Bazar, Lahore.</p>\n        ",
