            "title": "Early Sultanate Architecture: Quwwat-ul-Islam & Qutb Minar",
            "summary": "Development of Indo-Islamic architecture, reuse of temple elements (corbelled trabeate to arcuated style), Quwwat-ul-Islam Mosque, Qutb Minar, and Adhai Din Ka Jhonpra at Ajmer.",
            "theoryHtml": "\n        <h4>The Genesis of Indo-Islamic Architecture</h4>\n        <p>The establishment of the Delhi Sultanate introduced architectural traditions developed in Central Asia and Persia—domes, true arches, vaults, and minarets—to the Indian subcontinent, where indigenous builders worked in the traditional <em>trabeate</em> (post-and-lintel) style:</p>\n        <ul>\n            <li>Early Turkish conquerors lacked their own master masons, employing native Indian stonecutters and incorporating salvaged pillars, brackets, and lintels from existing Hindu and Jain shrines.</li>\n            <li>This initial phase is characterized by <strong>spolia reuse</strong> with Islamic decorative calligraphy, geometric patterns (arabesque), and floral arabesques layered over Indian carvings.</li>\n        </ul>\n\n        <h4>Quwwat-ul-Islam Mosque (Delhi, 1192–1198 AD)</h4>\n        <p>Constructed by Qutbuddin Aibak in the citadel of <em>Qila Rai Pithora</em> (Mehrauli, Delhi), this was the first congregational Friday mosque erected in northern India:</p>\n        <ul>\n            <li>According to Aibak's inscription on the eastern gateway, the mosque was constructed using the architectural materials and carved stone pillars dismantled from <strong>27 Hindu and Jain temples</strong>.</li>\n            <li>In 1199 AD, an imposing stone arched screen (<em>Maqsura</em>) was erected in front of the prayer chamber, featuring five corbelled pointed arches inscribed with verses from the Quran and delicate serpentine vine motifs.</li>\n            <li>In the courtyard stands the famous 4th-century CE rust-resistant <strong>Iron Pillar of King Chandra</strong> (Gupta period), retained as a trophy of conquest.</li>\n        </ul>\n\n        <h4>The Monumental Qutb Minar</h4>\n        <p>Commenced by Qutbuddin Aibak in <strong>1199 AD</strong> and completed by Shamsuddin Iltutmish (who added three storeys) around 1220 AD:</p>\n        <ul>\n            <li><strong>Inspiration & Dedication:</strong> Built in memory of the celebrated Sufi saint <strong>Khwaja Qutbuddin Bakhtiyar Kaki</strong>, while also serving as a victory tower and minaret for the call to prayer (<em>Azan</em>).</li>\n            <li><strong>Architectural Features:</strong> Rises to 72.5 meters (238 feet) across five storeys. The first storey features alternating angular and semi-circular fluting; the second is entirely round; the third is completely angular. Balconies are supported by intricate corbelled stalactite honeycombing.</li>\n            <li>In 1369 AD, lightning damaged the top storey. Sultan <strong>Firoz Shah Tughlaq</strong> repaired it, replacing the damaged portion with two smaller storeys using white marble and red sandstone.</li>\n        </ul>\n\n        <h4>Adhai Din Ka Jhonpra (Ajmer, c. 1199 AD)</h4>\n        <p>Commissioned by Aibak at Ajmer after the annexation of the Chauhan capital:</p>\n        <ul>\n            <li>Constructed on the site of a renowned Sanskrit college (<em>Saraswati Kanthabharana</em>) founded by Chauhan King Vigraharaja IV (Visaladeva). Inscriptions containing portions of Vigraharaja's Sanskrit drama <em>Harakeli Nataka</em> were discovered embedded in the building's stones.</li>\n            <li>Tradition ascribes the name 'Adhai Din Ka Jhonpra' (Shed of Two-and-a-Half Days) to either its rapid initial conversion in 60 hours or a later two-and-a-half-day Sufi Urs fair held on the premises.</li>\n            <li>Iltutmish later added an exquisite red sandstone screen consisting of seven pointed arches with perforated screens and calligraphic inscriptions.</li>\n        </ul>\n        ",
